1
00:00:00,820 --> 00:00:03,102
I added one line to a script on purpose:

2
00:00:03,682 --> 00:00:07,484
a made-up machine name, a made-up
address and a made-up port.

3
00:00:08,425 --> 00:00:13,027
If that line had reached the voice, my
voice clone would have read it out loud.

4
00:00:13,928 --> 00:00:16,569
Instead, the safety check
caught it before any recording,

5
00:00:17,129 --> 00:00:20,771
and it told me which line and
what kind of thing it had found.

6
00:00:21,672 --> 00:00:24,573
A word you do not notice is
the one that ends up public,

7
00:00:25,174 --> 00:00:27,455
and that check only knows
what it has been taught.

8
00:00:28,536 --> 00:00:31,037
So most of this part is
about what comes before it:

9
00:00:31,637 --> 00:00:34,819
what a video says, where
each fact comes from,

10
00:00:35,259 --> 00:00:36,340
and what must never appear.

11
00:00:38,601 --> 00:00:41,282
This part of the series takes
those choices in order:

12
00:00:41,862 --> 00:00:44,223
the one-page plan, the fact sheet,

13
00:00:44,763 --> 00:00:47,585
the script, and then the
checks that read all of it.

14
00:00:48,905 --> 00:00:52,947
Each one is small, and each gives
the next something to rely on.

15
00:00:54,107 --> 00:00:59,510
Before any script exists, you decide what
one video says, and I will start there.

16
00:01:01,130 --> 00:01:07,371
Start with the plan, because a video that tries to
answer three questions answers none of them well.

17
00:01:08,871 --> 00:01:11,652
The plan is one page,
written before the script.

18
00:01:12,872 --> 00:01:17,152
It holds a thesis, one sentence a
viewer could repeat afterwards;

19
00:01:17,733 --> 00:01:20,333
the audience, with the
question they come in with;

20
00:01:20,893 --> 00:01:23,753
and a short list of the
facts the thesis depends on.

21
00:01:25,354 --> 00:01:26,834
Then comes the part people skip:

22
00:01:27,454 --> 00:01:29,134
what this episode will not cover,

23
00:01:29,714 --> 00:01:32,395
so each of those items can
become a later episode.

24
00:01:33,815 --> 00:01:38,195
Last, it sketches the scenes, each
with its one point and a bridge,

25
00:01:38,776 --> 00:01:41,516
a sentence that says why
it follows the one before.

26
00:01:42,976 --> 00:01:44,776
You do not start from a blank page:

27
00:01:45,376 --> 00:01:51,217
one command, new, sets up an episode folder with
this page and empty versions of the other files.

28
00:01:52,877 --> 00:01:55,278
The plan names the facts
your thesis depends on,

29
00:01:55,858 --> 00:01:58,559
and the fact sheet is where
each one earns its place.

30
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:05,082
The rule is simple: the script and the
scenes may take a fact only from this one

31
00:02:05,182 --> 00:02:07,763
file, and every fact carries a marker.

32
00:02:09,283 --> 00:02:13,885
A cited fact has a public source, and you
note the link and the day you read it.

33
00:02:15,266 --> 00:02:20,447
A measured fact comes from your own test,
so you note how, when, and how many runs.

34
00:02:21,888 --> 00:02:24,429
An estimated fact is your own best guess:

35
00:02:24,989 --> 00:02:28,050
you note what it rests on,
and the script says about.

36
00:02:29,051 --> 00:02:31,752
A marker is only as good
as the reading behind it,

37
00:02:32,352 --> 00:02:37,054
so read the source itself, and write the
claim no stronger than the source is.

38
00:02:38,434 --> 00:02:43,016
A search snippet, a forum post or a
model's answer is a lead, not a source.

39
00:02:44,596 --> 00:02:46,938
Even a well-sourced fact can go stale,

40
00:02:47,538 --> 00:02:50,480
because a video stays up
long after a number moves.

41
00:02:51,921 --> 00:02:54,923
So for anything you measured,
say the date in the video,

42
00:02:55,423 --> 00:02:58,625
out loud and on screen:
measured on this date.

43
00:03:00,346 --> 00:03:02,908
Then a later change does
not make the video false;

44
00:03:03,488 --> 00:03:06,811
it makes it dated, and you
can see which part to redo.

45
00:03:08,131 --> 00:03:10,793
In the same spirit, an
estimate always says about,

46
00:03:11,373 --> 00:03:14,495
and anything you could not
verify goes under a heading,

47
00:03:15,096 --> 00:03:17,898
not claimed, so nobody
adds it back by accident.

48
00:03:19,338 --> 00:03:21,978
Some facts are true and
still must not be said,

49
00:03:22,578 --> 00:03:26,179
which is why the next step is to
sort the facts into two piles.

50
00:03:27,659 --> 00:03:31,180
Everything the video may use
is rewritten in generic words:

51
00:03:31,740 --> 00:03:34,160
every machine name, address, port,

52
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:37,900
room, place and person
becomes what it does,

53
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:40,481
such as the machine
with the graphics card.

54
00:03:42,261 --> 00:03:46,661
What you will never publish goes in a
separate file outside the episode folder,

55
00:03:47,262 --> 00:03:50,222
and you add its name to your
git ignore file yourself,

56
00:03:50,802 --> 00:03:53,122
because nothing here does that for you.

57
00:03:54,862 --> 00:03:58,163
Then every private name goes on
your list of names to block,

58
00:03:58,743 --> 00:04:01,543
the denylist, which the
safety check will use.

59
00:04:02,983 --> 00:04:05,844
One trap: do not list a
name you show on purpose,

60
00:04:06,244 --> 00:04:10,064
such as your channel name, or
every episode fails its own check.

61
00:04:11,504 --> 00:04:15,086
With the facts sorted, the script
can use only the public pile,

62
00:04:15,666 --> 00:04:17,427
and its format is a few rules.

63
00:04:18,867 --> 00:04:21,628
Each heading with two hash
marks starts a scene.

64
00:04:22,949 --> 00:04:26,290
The paragraphs under it are
exactly what the voice will say.

65
00:04:27,270 --> 00:04:31,632
A line that starts with the visual marker
is a note for whoever draws that scene;

66
00:04:32,212 --> 00:04:34,953
it is never spoken and
never becomes a subtitle.

67
00:04:36,074 --> 00:04:41,516
A spoken-form tag fixes a misread word,
and a pause marker gives exact silence.

68
00:04:43,137 --> 00:04:48,339
The validate command only checks that every
scene has narration and a title of its

69
00:04:48,459 --> 00:04:51,460
own, and warns when the
length is off your target.

70
00:04:53,221 --> 00:04:56,262
It cannot tell whether a scene
connects to the one before.

71
00:04:57,582 --> 00:05:01,244
That is a reviewer's job, and it
is the rule that matters most:

72
00:05:01,824 --> 00:05:05,145
every scene starts by tying
itself to the previous one.

73
00:05:06,726 --> 00:05:11,407
Everything so far is text, and text is
exactly what the safety check reads,

74
00:05:11,887 --> 00:05:13,547
so now it can do its work.

75
00:05:14,787 --> 00:05:18,008
It runs before the voice,
the pictures and the render,

76
00:05:18,608 --> 00:05:20,528
and again before every upload.

77
00:05:21,928 --> 00:05:24,668
It reads every text file
in the episode folder,

78
00:05:25,249 --> 00:05:29,009
and when the frames are made, the
words the scenes actually draw.

79
00:05:30,549 --> 00:05:33,410
When it finds something, it
prints the file and line,

80
00:05:33,890 --> 00:05:36,730
a hint, and a sample
with most of it hidden,

81
00:05:37,330 --> 00:05:39,850
so the report never
repeats the whole secret.

82
00:05:40,711 --> 00:05:43,191
Until your denylist has
your own names in it,

83
00:05:43,791 --> 00:05:48,252
every command that runs the check prints
a loud banner of exclamation marks.

84
00:05:49,592 --> 00:05:53,232
And once the banner is gone,
that only means the list exists,

85
00:05:53,792 --> 00:05:55,213
not that it is complete.

86
00:05:56,952 --> 00:05:58,373
The check guards what you make,

87
00:05:58,993 --> 00:06:01,395
but not what you save into
your project's history,

88
00:06:01,975 --> 00:06:04,736
so two small git hooks guard that step.

89
00:06:05,837 --> 00:06:08,278
You turn them on once, with one command.

90
00:06:09,519 --> 00:06:12,340
The first refuses a short
list of files by name:

91
00:06:12,941 --> 00:06:15,162
your settings, your
list of names to block,

92
00:06:15,682 --> 00:06:20,004
your voice files, and anything
with an audio or video extension.

93
00:06:21,325 --> 00:06:26,747
It never reads what is inside a file, so
the check above is still your text check.

94
00:06:28,288 --> 00:06:31,630
The second deletes one kind of
line from a commit message,

95
00:06:32,210 --> 00:06:36,032
the one an AI coding assistant
adds to link back to its chat.

96
00:06:37,693 --> 00:06:40,674
Every other line stays, so
read the message yourself,

97
00:06:41,255 --> 00:06:43,256
and know that skipping
the hooks skips both.

98
00:06:44,776 --> 00:06:49,318
None of this is complete, because the
check only knows what you have taught it,

99
00:06:49,918 --> 00:06:51,398
and it only matches patterns.

100
00:06:52,899 --> 00:06:57,220
It does not know your street or your
employer unless they are on the list.

101
00:06:58,721 --> 00:07:01,222
It cannot catch a place
described in words,

102
00:07:01,802 --> 00:07:04,263
a face, a reflection in a window,

103
00:07:04,843 --> 00:07:07,564
or the terminal line that shows
your user and machine name.

104
00:07:08,844 --> 00:07:10,765
So you look at every frame yourself,

105
00:07:11,345 --> 00:07:14,746
and you run the picture text
scan by hand on every recording;

106
00:07:15,346 --> 00:07:19,128
it needs an extra tool, and
without it, it checks nothing.

107
00:07:20,708 --> 00:07:22,669
That is the whole plan for an episode:

108
00:07:23,249 --> 00:07:25,369
a one-page plan, a fact sheet,

109
00:07:25,449 --> 00:07:29,530
a script, and a check that reads all
of it before anything is recorded.

110
00:07:30,811 --> 00:07:32,091
That is the end of this part.

111
00:07:33,071 --> 00:07:35,572
In the next part, we start
on the script itself:

112
00:07:36,152 --> 00:07:41,313
writing for the ear, with a reviewer who reads
it as a listener who cannot see the headings.
